  "technical_reviews": [
    {
      "zone": "FINAL_AST",
      "verdict_semantically_adjudicated_by_main_agent_not_keyword_parser": "changes-requested-before-correction",
      "model": "gpt-6-luna",
      "session": "01a17ef2-acdd-70cb-84c3-ff62751d1bfd",
      "answer_sha256": "df0502b816fc35bbf7b6dcc5e842470df786f268eb5e80fb9b6549402279e370",
      "answer_verbatim": "**CHANGES_REQUESTED**\n\n- **Required \u2014 `src/deadcode_audit/javascript_functions.py:163-168, 297-308`**: `var` bindings always climb to the nearest function scope, but JavaScript class static initialization blocks are their own `var` scope. The current scope model does not preserve that boundary. For example, inside a function containing `class C { static { var fetch = 1; } }`, a later `fetch()` should remain a free API reference; this code resolves it as local. Replacing `fetch` with `request` can therefore produce identical fingerprints and erase the API distinction. Model the static-block `var` boundary and add a regression test asserting those references remain distinct; adding the node only as a lexical block is insufficient while `var` still climbs to the function scope.\n\nThe reviewed grammar-based extraction, source-span handling, parse-failure behavior, generator/method binding cases, and parser-version guard otherwise look sound. I did not run tests or models.",
      "source_only_not_owner_acceptance": true
    },
    {
      "zone": "FINAL_PIPELINE",
      "verdict_semantically_adjudicated_by_main_agent_not_keyword_parser": "accepted-no-required-changes",
      "model": "gpt-6-luna",
      "session": "01a17ef2-acf0-77f6-a7fa-a24e0b4f1b8c",
      "answer_sha256": "361d53174da69c9ac9d3ab876f0311f8c63768ca2b0f0163bf68919d88e836ad",
      "answer_verbatim": "**ACCEPTED \u2014 no required changes.**\n\nThe scan/CI integration reuses the existing `Diagnostic`, severity overrides, scoring, and output path; the Oxlint adapter pins the protocol and fails closed on missing or invalid backend responses (`src/deadcode_audit/javascript.py:27-48,176-260`; `src/deadcode_audit/scan.py:99-107`). Optional extras keep the parser and model dependencies out of the base install.\n\nThe embedding requirement is met: I inspected the raw JS and mixed reports, not just the summary. They show **425 JS functions, 314 actually embedded, 1,743 candidate pairs**; mixed input shows **617 functions, 487 embedded (173 Python + 314 JS), 2,035 pairs**, with CodeBERT revision and per-function source hashes/spans. The original Python report remains byte-identical. The 111 JS functions below the default token threshold are explicitly ineligible\u2014not implied coverage. These are overlap candidates, not accuracy evidence or deletion permission (`src/deadcode-audit/docs/JAVASCRIPT.md:86-100`).\n\nThe quality findings are appropriately framed: 39 unused-binding warnings include catch parameters; 22 empty-block warnings can reflect intentional fallback/error handling. They are not 61 confirmed dead-code defects. Unknown dynamic consumers and exports are not classified as dead.\n\n**Nonblocking follow-up:** `src/deadcode_audit/overlaps.py:75-78` applies configured excludes to default-discovered JS files but not Python files. This preserves prior Python behavior; clarify whether `exclude` is intended to filter default overlap corpora.\n\nRead-only review; I did not run tests.",
      "source_only_not_owner_acceptance": true
    },
    {
      "zone": "STATIC_CORRECTION",
      "verdict_semantically_adjudicated_by_main_agent_not_keyword_parser": "accepted-no-required-changes",
      "model": "gpt-6-luna",
      "session": "01a17eff-0900-7750-9e4b-9b2c79bd394a",
      "answer_sha256": "08099afa749e85b3ac5ff6583320945d0e1785ca75aedb072b46522b9d52a148",
      "answer_verbatim": "**ACCEPTED \u2014 no required changes.**\n\n`src/deadcode_audit/javascript_functions.py:163-166,244-246,308` correctly makes a class static block its own `var` scope: `var_scope` stops at `static` as well as `function`, and the grammar node creates that scope. Ordinary block scopes still climb to the enclosing function. This matches the static-block semantics in the retained MDN and ESLint sources.\n\nThe JS/TS regression at `tests/test_javascript_functions.py:201-213` asserts that outer `fetch` and `request` remain distinct free references, while ordinary-block `var` hoisting remains intact. These are deterministic IR contracts, not language-grading evidence; I did not run tests or models.\n\nI found no additional blocker in the reviewed pipeline path. The prior exclusion clarification is now explicit in `docs/JAVASCRIPT.md:78-80` and matches `src/deadcode_audit/overlaps.py:71-78`: explicit targets exclude both languages, default JS honors excludes, and default Python retains historical behavior. Read-only review; no edits or execution.",
      "source_only_not_owner_acceptance": true
    }
